export const lpContent={
 'rabbit-meadow':{
  title:'The Rabbit',one:'Why a soft grassland full of tall growth is exactly the home a digging, nibbling, hiding animal needs.',
  seoTitle:'Rabbit Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Meadow Homes, Hiding and Burrows (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a meadow is, why the tall grass feeds and hides a rabbit at once, and a crouch-down game that shows the meadow at rabbit height.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what a meadow actually is and why it suits the rabbit perfectly: dinner and a hiding place growing from the same soil, plus a burrow underneath for the night.',
  what:'A meadow is a field of tall grass and wildflowers that is allowed to grow long. No pavements, no lawn mower — just grass, stems, seeds and the small tunnels underneath.',
  mean:'For a rabbit the tall growth does two jobs at the same time. It is food — rabbits nibble grass almost all day — and it is cover: a rabbit low in long grass is nearly invisible from above. Home that feeds you and hides you is a good home.',
  know:'Your child needs three meadow ideas: meadows are fields of tall grass, not empty gaps; the grass is the rabbit\u2019s dinner and its hiding spot in one; and underneath it all the rabbit digs a burrow — a tunnel home where it sleeps and raises its babies.',
  why:'The rabbit is the friendliest possible door into habitats, because children already know rabbits from stories. The discovery is that the rabbit in the book cannot actually live anywhere: it needs the meadow, specifically, and once a child sees that, every other animal-home pairing in this class starts making sense.',
  dev:'Habitat matching (animal to home), the idea that bodies suit their homes — big ears for hearing danger, strong back legs for hopping away — plus empathy through hiding play, and the first green word every ecologist needs: grassland.',
  vocab:'meadow, grass, tall, hide, burrow, nibble, hop',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a rabbit, and this is its home — a meadow."\n2) Say the two-job idea slowly: the tall grass is the rabbit\u2019s food AND its hiding place. One plant, both jobs.\n3) Point at the picture: where could the burrow be? Under the green mound — the rabbit\u2019s front door is a hole.\n4) Ask what the rabbit\u2019s big ears and big back legs are for. Hearing danger early, and hopping away fast — the meadow rewards both.\n5) Finish with the crouch game (below) and one sentence to take away: "the rabbit lives in the meadow because the meadow feeds it and hides it."',
  say:'"The rabbit lives in the meadow. The tall grass is its lunch and its hiding place at the same time — and underneath, the rabbit has dug a burrow where it sleeps. Nobody can see it from up high. That is why the meadow is the right home for a rabbit."',
  notice:'On this card a soft beige rabbit with pink-lined ears and a tiny smile sits calmly on a round green mound of grass. Behind it the sky is plain and pale, and the word "meadow" sits below — the rabbit is outdoors, low, and surrounded by exactly the green it eats.',
  ask:'"What does the rabbit eat in the meadow?" "Where does the grass hide the rabbit from?" "Where does the rabbit sleep at night — in a bed, or a hole?" "What are those big ears for?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The grass is the rabbit\u2019s food," "It hides in the tall grass so nobody sees it," or the burrow line: "It sleeps in a hole under the ground!"',
  real:'Find any unmown strip — a park edge, a roadside verge, a corner of the garden — and crouch down inside it. From rabbit height the grass is a roof overhead. That little flip of scale, forest of grass, is the meadow understood.',
  play:'Hide-the-rabbit: one player closes their eyes while the "rabbit" curls small behind grass-colored things or under a green blanket. The seeker gets one look from standing height only — then must find the rabbit by walking low. Hiding works better low. That is the whole meadow lesson, played.',
  tips:'If your child worries the rabbit is lonely or scared out there, lean in: the burrow has a whole family in it. Meadow, food, family underground — rabbits live a small, warm life that children find very satisfying once it is described.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does a rabbit live? What are the two jobs of the tall grass? Where does it sleep?" Meadow, food-and-hiding, burrow — three answers and the lesson is home.'},
 'frog-pond':{
  title:'The Frog',one:'The pond as a frog\u2019s whole world — seat, snack bar, swimming pool and nursery in one stretch of still water.',
  seoTitle:'Frog Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Pond Habitats, Lily Pads and Tadpoles (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a pond is, why frogs live half in and half out of the water, how tadpoles grow, and a plop-and-sit game to play at home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what a pond is and why the frog is the animal that fits it best: a hunter that sits on the lily pads, swims when trouble comes, and raises its babies in the same quiet water.',
  what:'A pond is a small body of still water — smaller than a lake, big enough for lily pads, reeds, tadpoles and all the insects frogs eat. Ponds can be tiny: some fit in a garden.',
  mean:'The frog is a two-home animal in one home: it breathes air and sits on the pads, but it swims like a champion and lays its eggs in the water. The pond feeds it flies, hides it underwater and raises its tadpoles. Every part of the frog\u2019s day happens within one splash of the edge.',
  know:'Your child needs three pond ideas: ponds are small still waters with plants growing in and around them; frogs sit on lily pads by day and plop into the water when startled; and frog babies are tadpoles — little swimming dots that grow legs and turn into frogs right there in the pond.',
  why:'The frog delivers the strangest, most memorable fact in the early habitat curriculum: it completely changes its body as it grows, from swimming tadpole to hopping frog — and it does it all inside one pond. Children who meet that transformation never forget that water is a living place.',
  dev:'Life-cycle sequencing (egg, tadpole, frog), the two-worlds idea of air and water, listening skills through croak-spotting, and gentle nerve — ponds are slimy, jumpy places and approaching them anyway is real confidence work.',
  vocab:'pond, lily pad, reed, croak, plop, tadpole, swim',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a frog, and this is its home — a pond."\n2) Read the scene: the frog sits ON the water, not in it — that flat green pad is its chair. Ask what happens if a heron comes near. Plop! Straight into the water.\n3) Tell the baby story: frog eggs are laid in the pond, tiny tadpoles hatch and swim, and slowly they grow legs and hop out. The pond is the nursery.\n4) Practice the frog move: crouch small on an imaginary pad, sit very still, then leap and "plop" behind a cushion.\n5) Finish with the listening challenge — next pond visit, the game is to hear one croak.',
  say:'"The frog lives at the pond. It sits on the lily pad very, very still, and when anything comes close — plop! — into the water it goes. The pond is where it eats, swims and where it was a tadpole once, with no legs at all."',
  notice:'On this card a bright green frog with a wide smile and big dark eyes sits in the middle of a round lily pad. Two orange-tipped reeds stand at its sides and gentle green water spreads below. The word "pond" sits under the scene — everything the frog needs fits in this one still corner.',
  ask:'"Where does the frog sit?" "What does the frog do when someone comes close?" "What was the frog before it was a frog?" "Can we be that still?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It sits on the lily pad," "Plop! It jumps in the water," or the life-cycle line: "It was a tadpole first — it swims and then it hops!"',
  real:'Any pond, park puddle-pool or even a fountain edge works for frog-spotting: approach on tiptoe, listen for croaks, scan the pads. Most city ponds have frogs; the tiptoe approach is what makes it work. If a real plop happens as you arrive — that WAS the frog.',
  play:'Plop-and-freeze: everyone is a frog on a cushion pad. One player is the heron, walking slow circles. When the heron looks your way — freeze on the pad. When it looks away — you may bob and ribbit. Caught mid-bob means you hop to a new pad. Stillness is the skill; giggling is allowed after.',
  tips:'Do not rush the tadpole story — it is the single best transformation children this age own, and retelling it in three scenes (egg, swimmer, hopper) builds narrative muscle. And if your child asks whether frogs sleep in the water: yes, half-in, half-out — a frog never really leaves the pond far behind.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does a frog live? Where does it sit? What do we call its swimming babies?" Pond, lily pad, tadpoles — three answers and the lesson has landed.',
  safety:'Pond edges are slip-prone: at real water, one grown-up holds one small hand, always. Never lean over a pond alone, and wash hands after any pond-side play — frogs are lovely, pond water is not for fingers.'},
 'squirrel-woodland':{
  title:'The Squirrel',one:'The woodland as a pantry that must be filled before winter — hiding food high and low and (sometimes) planting forests by mistake.',
  seoTitle:'Squirrel Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Woodland Homes, Nut Storing and Winter Prep (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what woodland is, how squirrels gather and hide nuts for winter, why forgotten acorns grow into trees, and a gather-and-remember game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what woodland is and how the squirrel earns its living there: climbing, collecting and caching — and how the squirrel\u2019s forgetfulness quietly grows the next forest.',
  what:'Woodland is a landscape of many trees with open, airy ground between them — lighter and bushier than deep forest. Acorns, seeds and mushrooms grow there, and tree trunks stand like apartment towers.',
  mean:'For the squirrel the woodland is a supermarket that closes in winter. All autumn it gathers nuts and hides them in hundreds of separate spots — and in winter it digs them up again. The trees feed the squirrel; the squirrel spreads the trees. Two neighbours helping each other without knowing it.',
  know:'Your child needs three woodland ideas: woodland is tree-country with food on the ground; squirrels prepare for winter by hiding nuts in many secret places; and some hidden nuts are never found again — those grow into new trees.',
  why:'The squirrel teaches foresight — work now, eat later — which is a genuinely new idea for four-year-olds, delivered by a twitchy, charming animal. The accidental-forester twist adds a cause-and-effect gem: today\u2019s lost acorn is next century\u2019s oak.',
  dev:'Planning and future thinking ("winter is coming — gather now"), spatial memory through hiding games, tree-part vocabulary (trunk, branch, acorn), and the mutual-help idea that runs all through nature.',
  vocab:'woodland, acorn, gather, hide, winter, trunk, stash',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a squirrel, and this is its home — the woodland."\n2) Point at the acorn in the paws: the squirrel is holding its winter plans. In autumn it hides hundreds of nuts — not one big pile, but hundreds of little secret stashes.\n3) Tell the twist: the squirrel forgets some of them. Those forgotten nuts sleep in the ground and wake up as baby trees. Squirrels plant forests by accident.\n4) Ask where the squirrel lives in the trees — up in a drey, a round leafy nest, high in the branches.\n5) Play the gather-and-remember game (below), then close with the cycle: tree makes nuts, squirrel hides nuts, forgotten nuts make new trees.',
  say:'"The squirrel lives in the woodland. All autumn it gathers acorns and hides them in secret places for winter. It forgets some of them — and those grow into new trees! The squirrel is the forest\u2019s little gardener without knowing it."',
  notice:'On this card a red-brown squirrel with a cream chest and tufted ears holds one acorn in both front paws, sitting beside a thick brown trunk with a leafy branch overhead. The word "woodland" sits below — tree, squirrel and acorn, the whole partnership in one scene.',
  ask:'"What is the squirrel holding?" "Why does it hide nuts instead of eating them all now?" "What happens to the nuts it forgets?" "Where up in the tree does it sleep?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It hides acorns for winter," "It forgot some — they grow into trees!" or the proud memory claim: "I can find all five of mine!"',
  real:'Under any big tree in autumn, hunt for acorns and half-eaten stashes — teeth marks on a dropped acorn are squirrel evidence. In parks, watch real squirrels dig, pause, and dig elsewhere: a cache being checked, or a fake burial to fool watchers.',
  play:'Gather-and-remember: hide five "acorns" around a room while your child watches, then send the little squirrel to gather them all into one stash. Next round, your child hides and you gather. Hardest round: hide them, play something else for ten minutes, then search — winter is long and squirrels\u2019 memories are tested by time.',
  tips:'Let your child be better than the squirrel — remembering all five is a real triumph worth a cheer. The forget-some part is not failure; it is the forest\u2019s plan. Keep that framing warm: mistakes that grow trees are the best kind.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does a squirrel live? What does it do all autumn? What grows from its forgotten nuts?" Woodland, hides nuts, new trees — three answers and the pantry is stocked.'},
 'fox-woodland':{
  title:'The Fox',one:'Life at the woodland\u2019s quiet edges — the night-shift neighbour who sleeps by day and trots the shadows by dusk.',
  seoTitle:'Fox Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Woodland Edges, Dens and Quiet Feet (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: where a fox makes its den, why it hunts at dusk, how its ears and quiet feet work, and a silent-steps game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches how the fox uses the woodland differently from the squirrel: same trees, opposite timetable. The fox sleeps through the day and owns the quiet hours — with ears that hear everything and feet that make no sound at all.',
  what:'A fox lives where the woodland meets the open: hedgerows, thickets, bramble corners — good hiding near good hunting. Its den is a hole or a sheltered hollow where cubs are born and days are slept away.',
  mean:'Two animals can share one woodland by working different shifts. The squirrel works all day; the fox starts at dusk. Big triangle ears catch the smallest squeak in the grass, and paws built for silence let the fox arrive before anything hears it. The woodland offers night and day — the fox took the night.',
  know:'Your child needs three fox ideas: foxes make dens in quiet corners at the woodland edge; they are most active at dusk and dawn, not midday; and their tools are giant ears, sharp eyes and feet so soft that nothing hears them coming.',
  why:'The fox gently breaks a pattern: not every animal\u2019s day looks like ours. Meeting an animal whose "morning" is our bedtime stretches a child\u2019s imagination of time itself — and the fox\u2019s famous cleverness becomes concrete when it is explained as ears plus patience plus quiet feet.',
  dev:'Time concepts (day shift and night shift), sound awareness through listening games, body-tool reasoning (ears, eyes, feet), and gentle courage — foxes are the animals children both love and find just a little bit spooky, and understanding dissolves the spooky.',
  vocab:'fox, den, dusk, quiet, trot, bushy, sharp ears',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a fox, and this is where it lives — the edge of the woodland."\n2) Compare shifts: the squirrel naps at night; the fox naps by day and goes trotting when the sky turns orange. Same forest, different clock.\n3) Point at the ears: those triangles catch the tiniest sounds — a mouse\u2019s squeak from far away in the grass.\n4) Do the ears test right now: whisper from across the room and watch how fast your child\u2019s fox ears find you.\n5) Finish with silent feet practice — fox-walk across the room without one creak. If someone hears you, back to the den and try again.',
  say:'"The fox lives at the woodland edge, in a den among the bushes. It sleeps in the day, and when the sun goes down it trots out on soft, silent feet. Its big ears hear everything — that is how the fox feeds its family at night."',
  notice:'On this card an orange fox with a white chest, black ear-tips and black boots sits calmly in short green grass, a pine tree and round bushes behind it, under a pale blue sky. The word "woodland" sits below — the fox is resting between its night shifts, right at the trees\u2019 soft edge.',
  ask:'"When does the fox wake up — morning or evening?" "How do its feet help it hunt?" "What do those big ears hear?" "Where does a fox sleep through the day?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The fox sleeps in the day," "Its feet are so quiet nobody hears it," or the ears line: "It hears a mouse far away with those big ears!"',
  real:'At dusk, open a window and listen for fox sounds — a short sharp bark carries far in the evening quiet. In towns too: foxes live along garden edges and railway lines. Early morning paw prints in soft ground tell last night\u2019s trotting route.',
  play:'Fox-and-feathers: one player is the fox and must cross the room and lift a toy from a shelf without the "rabbits" (lying on the floor, eyes closed) hearing a single step. Any twitch of the rabbits\u2019 ears sends the fox back to the den. Silence is the whole game — and the whole fox.',
  tips:'If your child finds foxes a bit frightening, lead with competence, not creepiness: the fox is not sneaky for fun, it is a parent bringing food home to cubs in a den. A tired working parent with excellent ears is a very friendly monster to be.',
  check:'Ask: "When is the fox busy — day or night? How does it walk? What do its ears do?" Night, silent feet, hear everything — three answers and the den is warm.'},
 'owl-forest':{
  title:'The Owl',one:'The forest from the top branch down — huge eyes, silent wings and a bird that rules the hours when everyone else is asleep.',
  seoTitle:'Owl Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Forest Layers, Silent Flight and Night Eyes (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: where an owl perches in the forest, why its eyes are so big, how it flies without a sound, and a listen-and-swoop game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the owl\u2019s version of forest life: perched high above the floor, watching with the biggest eyes in the bird world and gliding down through the dark without a whisper of wing.',
  what:'A forest is deeper and darker than woodland — tall trunks close together, layered from floor to canopy. Owls claim the middle-height branches, and older trees offer hollows: ready-made dark rooms for sleeping the day away.',
  mean:'The forest stacks life in floors: roots below, moss and mice on the ground, branches in the middle, leaves on top. The owl rents the middle floor and hunts the bottom one — from a branch with a clear view, its giant eyes gather light so well that night looks like late evening up there. When it hears a move below, it drops silently and the forest floor never hears it coming.',
  know:'Your child needs three owl ideas: owls perch on branches and rest in tree hollows by day; their huge eyes see in near-dark, and they turn their whole heads to look around; and their wing feathers are fringed so soft that flight makes no sound at all.',
  why:'The owl reframes "night is scary" into "night has its own experts". Children meet the dark through an animal that is not just unafraid but spectacularly good at it — the forest at night becomes a workplace with the owl on shift, and bedtime stops being when the world ends.',
  dev:'Vertical thinking (habitat layers: floor, branch, canopy), light-and-dark science, head-turn body awareness, listening precision, and comfort with darkness built through the night-shift framing.',
  vocab:'owl, branch, hollow, glide, silent, twilight, hoot',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is an owl, and this is its home — high in the forest trees."\n2) Point at the eyes: those two big golden circles catch even the tiny light of the moon and stars. To the owl, night is never fully dark.\n3) Try the owl neck: eyes fixed forward, turn only your head slowly left and right. That is how owls look around — their eyes cannot swivel, so the head does the travelling.\n4) Do the silent-wing test with a scarf or tea towel: flap it fast (whoosh!) then slow and wide. Owls fly slow and wide — soft, silent, unbeatable for sneaking.\n5) Finish with the listen-and-swoop game (below) and one line: "the owl sleeps by day in a hollow and rules the forest at night."',
  say:'"The owl lives high in the forest. By day it rests in a hollow tree; by night its great golden eyes watch the ground below. Its wings make no sound at all — the mouse on the forest floor never hears a thing until the owl is there."',
  notice:'On this card a brown owl with a cream, speckled chest and two enormous golden eyes grips a leafy branch with its talons, dark pine trees standing behind it. The word "forest" sits below — perched, patient and facing us, the owl is exactly where a forest puts its night watch.',
  ask:'"Why are the owl\u2019s eyes so big?" "What sound do its wings make?" "Where does it sleep during the day?" "Can you turn your head like an owl?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Its eyes see in the dark," "Its wings are silent — whoosh, no sound!" or the head-turn boast: "I can look behind me like an owl!"',
  real:'At twilight, stand under tall trees and hoot-listen for one minute — many parks have owls that call right after sunset. By day, look for owl pellets and pale droppings on trunks below hollows: the quiet evidence of the night shift.',
  play:'Listen-and-swoop: the "mouse" rustles a paper under a blanket while the owl sits blindfolded (or eyes-closed) on a chair-branch. One rustle, then stillness — the owl points to where the sound came from, then swoops (arms wide, silent, no whooshing — owls never whoosh) to catch it. Score a point for silence on the swoop.',
  tips:'Own the head-turn yourself first — children copy the owl neck for days once it is taught, and every repetition rehearses the lesson. If the dark still feels big, end with this: somewhere out there, an owl is flying right now and it thinks the dark is easy.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the owl perch? What do its big eyes do? How do its wings fly?" On a branch, see in the dark, silently — three answers and the forest sleeps safe.'},
 'koala-eucalyptus-forest':{
  title:'The Koala',one:'The one-tree life — an animal that eats, sleeps and hangs its whole future on a single kind of tree.',
  seoTitle:'Koala Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Eucalyptus Forests and Specialist Animals (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a eucalyptus forest is, why koalas eat leaves that would make other animals sick, and a koala-hug game with a grip to match.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the most specialised home in the class: the koala lives in eucalyptus forest because eucalyptus leaves — poisonous to almost everyone else — are its one and only meal.',
  what:'A eucalyptus forest is a woodland of gum trees: smooth pale trunks, long silver-green leaves and a smell like cough sweets when the sun warms them. These trees grow across great stretches of Australia.',
  mean:'Eucalyptus leaves are full of strong oils — most animals that eat them get a very bad tummy. The koala\u2019s gut is built exactly for those oils, so a leaf that is poison to others is dinner to the koala. That one trick means nobody else wants its food, and its forest stays its own: one tree, one animal, one perfect match.',
  know:'Your child needs three koala ideas: eucalyptus trees have special leaves most animals cannot eat; the koala\u2019s tummy CAN — that is its superpower; and because its food is low-energy, the koala saves strength by sleeping up to twenty hours a day, hugging its branch.',
  why:'The koala is the class\u2019s cleanest specialist: where the rabbit eats many things and the fox many more, the koala eats one — and the lesson that bodies can be shaped to fit one home, one food, one place is the deep idea behind every habitat on Earth.',
  dev:'Specialist-versus-generalist reasoning (first form!), cause and effect (oily leaves, koala tummy), grip-strength body awareness, and the calm lesson that slow and sleepy is a strategy, not laziness.',
  vocab:'koala, eucalyptus, gum tree, leaves, hug, grip, sleepy',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a koala, and this is its home — the eucalyptus forest."\n2) Hold the superpower reveal for the big moment: these leaves would give most animals a terrible tummy — but the koala\u2019s tummy is built for exactly them. Poison to others, lunch to the koala.\n3) Explain the sleepy part: those leaves are not very filling, so the koala saves energy by sleeping nearly all day, hugging its branch high in the tree.\n4) Point at the paws on the card: two thumbs for gripping — made for hugging trunks.\n5) Do the koala hug (below), then close with the match: gum tree, koala tummy — made for each other.',
  say:'"The koala lives in the eucalyptus forest. Its leaves would make most animals sick, but the koala\u2019s tummy is built just for them. It eats, then it hugs a branch and sleeps — nearly the whole day. One tree is its whole world."',
  notice:'On this card a fluffy grey koala with big round ears and a black nose hugs a slanted eucalyptus branch, leafy sprigs rising on both sides of the picture. The words "eucalyptus forest" sit below — one animal, one tree, holding on.',
  ask:'"What does the koala eat?" "Could a fox eat these leaves?" "Why does the koala sleep so much?" "How many thumbs does a koala have on each paw?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It eats the leaves from the gum tree," "Other animals get sick but koalas don\u2019t!" or the sleepy line: "It sleeps almost all day hugging the tree."',
  real:'Crush one eucalyptus leaf near a florist\u2019s greens or a gum tree if you have one, and sniff — that strong medicine smell is the very oil that upsets other tummies. Koalas eat the smell. That connection lands harder than any explanation.',
  play:'Koala hug contest: everyone claims a "trunk" — a doorframe, a table leg, a grown-up — grips on with both hands and holds through a slow count of twenty without slipping. Winner gets the koala nap: ten seconds of seated dozing on the spot. Grip, then sleep. That is the daily koala routine, played.',
  tips:'Do not rush past "poison to others, dinner to the koala" — it is a jaw-dropper and deserves a pause. If your child then asks whether koalas are lazy, hold the line kindly: sleeping IS the koala\u2019s smart plan; it keeps the small food budget balanced.',
  check:'Ask: "Which tree is the koala\u2019s home? What do other animals think of its leaves? What does the koala do all day?" Eucalyptus, sick, sleep-and-hug — three answers and the lesson sticks like a koala on a branch.'},
 'panda-bamboo-forest':{
  title:'The Panda',one:'The misty mountain cafeteria — a big, gentle animal that spends nearly its whole day eating one crunchy green food.',
  seoTitle:'Panda Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Bamboo Forests and What a Panda Eats All Day (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a bamboo forest is, why the panda eats bamboo all day, how it grips the stalks, and a panda-snack game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what a bamboo forest is and how the panda lives in it: cool mountain air, green stalks everywhere, and a bear that turned eating leaves into a full-time job.',
  what:'A bamboo forest is a stand of bamboo — giant grasses as tall as trees, growing in thick green clusters high on cool, often misty mountains. Bamboo grows so fast it can almost be watched growing.',
  mean:'Bamboo is mostly water and fibre — filling, but not very rich. So the panda\u2019s strategy is volume: sit among the stalks and eat, for most waking hours, day after day. The forest never runs out, the panda never stops, and the misty mountains keep it all cool and green.',
  know:'Your child needs three bamboo-forest ideas: bamboo is giant grass, not a tree; pandas eat bamboo for breakfast, lunch and dinner — and most snacks between; and pandas have a special wrist bone that works like a thumb for gripping the stalks.',
  why:'The panda pairs beautifully with the koala: two specialists, two one-food lives, one cool forest and one warm one. Children meet the idea that a habitat is not just an address but a menu — and that a big strong bear can be a gentle, patient, all-day nibbler.',
  dev:'Diet thinking (what fills you up and why), contrast reasoning (bear expectation versus panda reality), grip mechanics, and pacing — the slow-and-steady eating marathon is a patience model children can feel at snack time.',
  vocab:'panda, bamboo, stalk, misty, mountains, munch, grip',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a panda, and this is its home — the bamboo forest."\n2) Reveal the surprise: this is a BEAR — big and strong — but it does not hunt. It munches grass. Giant grass: bamboo, tall as trees.\n3) Explain the all-day part: bamboo does not pack much power, so the panda eats and eats — most of its day is breakfast, lunch and dinner rolled together.\n4) Point at the front paws: a panda\u2019s wrist bone works like a sixth finger, made for gripping bamboo stalks.\n5) Play the panda snack (below) and close with the picture: cool mist, green towers, one contented bear munching.',
  say:'"The panda lives in the bamboo forest, high on the cool mountains. It is a big strong bear that eats bamboo all day — crunch, crunch, crunch. Its wrist has a special thumb just for holding the stalks."',
  notice:'On this card a round black-and-white panda walks on all fours across a green patch, tall bamboo stalks rising on every side under a soft pale sky. The words "bamboo forest" sit below — the panda is strolling through its own enormous salad bar.',
  ask:'"What is the panda eating?" "Is bamboo a tree or giant grass?" "How much of the panda\u2019s day is eating?" "What does its special wrist bone do?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It eats bamboo all day long," "Bamboo is giant grass — taller than Dad!" or the wrist line: "It has a thumb on its wrist for holding the bamboo!"',
  real:'At the fruit bowl, compare a juicy grape (rich, small) with a stick of celery (filling, plain) — celery is your household bamboo. One bite fills a little; a panda needs the whole forest. Rich foods make quick meals; plain foods make long ones.',
  play:'Panda picnic: serve a "bamboo" snack — breadsticks, celery sticks or green beans — and eat it panda-style: sitting cross-legged, stalk held in both front paws, chomped sideways from the end. Timer optional, but one stalk must be finished properly before any jumping. All-day eaters eat slowly.',
  tips:'Hold the contrast with other bears gently — yes, some bears hunt, but the panda found a forest full of food nobody else wanted and made it a kingdom. If your child asks why pandas do not eat something richer: the forest only serves bamboo, and the panda likes what its home serves.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the panda live? What does it eat? What helps it grip the stalks?" Bamboo forest, bamboo, the wrist-thumb — three answers and the picnic is packed.'},
 'monkey-rainforest':{
  title:'The Monkey',one:'Life above the ground — the rainforest canopy as a monkey\u2019s roads, playground and fruit market, all in the treetops.',
  seoTitle:'Monkey Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Rainforest Canopies and Treetop Life (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a rainforest is, why monkeys live up in the canopy, how troops work, and a don\u2019t-touch-the-floor climbing game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the rainforest as a layered world and the monkey as its treetop specialist: up in the canopy the light is good, the fruit is sweet and the floor belongs to somebody else entirely.',
  what:'A rainforest is a tall, thick, wet forest — the hottest, greenest habitat on Earth. Its trees stand so close and grow so high that they lock branches into a roof called the canopy, where most rainforest animals spend their whole lives.',
  mean:'Down on the forest floor it is dim, damp and hard to move; up in the canopy it is bright, fruity and full of highways — branches, vines and lianas laid out like roads. The monkey\u2019s whole design answers the canopy: gripping hands and feet, a balancing tail, eyes that judge every jump. The troop travels the treetops together, chattering news of the ripest fruit.',
  know:'Your child needs three rainforest ideas: rainforests are tall, wet, crowded forests full of life; the canopy — the treetop roof — is where monkeys live, not the ground; and monkeys move in troops, families that travel, eat and watch out for each other together.',
  why:'The rainforest gives children the single most useful habitat idea in the class: a home can have floors, and different animals live on different floors of the same forest. Once the canopy-versus-floor split is clear, the owl, the panda and every forest card after it gains a map.',
  dev:'Layer thinking (floor, middle, canopy), grip-and-balance body awareness, group-living concepts (troop, family, watch-out-for-each-other), and jumping judgement — the same skill a monkey\u2019s next branch demands.',
  vocab:'rainforest, canopy, treetop, vine, troop, grip, swing',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a monkey, and this is its home — the rainforest, up in the treetops."\n2) Build the layers with your hands: one hand low — dark, damp forest floor; other hand high — the canopy, where the sun gets through. The monkey lives up top.\n3) Point at the tail: curled like a hook. It balances and grips, a fifth hand for the high roads.\n4) Explain the troop: monkeys never travel alone — the family moves together, calls together and shares the fruit it finds.\n5) Play the canopy crossing (below) and close with the map: floor for the shy ones, canopy for the monkey.',
  say:'"The monkey lives in the rainforest — but not on the ground. Up in the treetops the branches are its roads and the fruit is its shop. It travels with its family, hand over hand, and its tail helps it hold on."',
  notice:'On this card a cheerful brown monkey with a lighter face and belly sits among deep green leaves, its long tail curling up behind it in a question-mark hook. The word "rainforest" sits below — deep in the green, well above the forest floor.',
  ask:'"Where does the rainforest monkey live — on the floor or in the trees?" "What is its tail for?" "Who does it travel with?" "What is the canopy?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It lives up in the trees," "Its tail holds on like a hook," or the troop line: "Monkeys go everywhere together, with their family!"',
  real:'Playground frames are canopy simulators — climbing them IS rainforest practice, and saying so mid-climb ("you are in the canopy right now!") turns the bars into a habitat. Vine and liana talk works at any rope or swing.',
  play:'Canopy crossing: tape a start and finish line and rule the floor off-limits — canopy travellers cross only by climbing, reaching and balancing along sofas, cushions and mats (grown-up spotting, of course). Touch the floor and it is a gentle "down to the forest floor — climb back up!" Reaching the far tree earns the fruit.',
  tips:'Name the troop habit out loud whenever the family walks anywhere together — "we are moving like a troop, nobody gets left behind." The rainforest lesson sticks to family life. And if a child asks what is DOWN there on the floor: the shy ones — anteaters, jaguars, beetles. Different floors, different families.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the monkey live in the rainforest? What is the treetop roof called? Who does it travel with?" In the trees, the canopy, its troop — three answers and the lesson swings home.'},
 'zebra-grassland':{
  title:'The Zebra',one:'Stripes that dazzle — how an open grass full of eyes is survived by wearing the boldest pattern in nature.',
  seoTitle:'Zebra Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Grassland Homes and the Zebra Stripe Trick (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a grassland is, how zebra stripes confuse hunters, why herds stick together, and a stripe-wave game that shows the dazzle.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what a grassland is and how the zebra survives one: no hiding places out in the open, so the zebra wears its hiding place — a moving crowd of stripes that melts every hunter\u2019s aim.',
  what:'A grassland is a huge stretch of grass with few trees and a sky that goes on forever — open, breezy country where grass grows taller than a toddler and herds can see trouble coming from far away.',
  mean:'Out on the grassland there is nowhere to hide — so the zebras hide in each other. When a herd stands and moves together, the stripes run together into one flickering pattern, and a hunter\u2019s eyes stop being able to say where one zebra ends and the next begins. The stripes also fit the light of tall grass: black, white and shadow-play.',
  know:'Your child needs three zebra ideas: grasslands are wide open grass countries with almost no trees; zebras live in herds and their stripes blur together when they move as one; and no two zebras share the same stripe pattern — each one is wearing its own name.',
  why:'The zebra turns a weakness (nothing to hide behind) into the set\u2019s most beautiful counter-trick, teaching children that safety can come from family togetherness as well as cover. It also plants the first "everyone is identifiable" idea: stripes like fingerprints.',
  dev:'Pattern and visual perception (what dazzles the eye), group behaviour (herd as shield), comparing open versus covered homes, and identity thinking — every zebra distinct, every pattern unique.',
  vocab:'zebra, grassland, herd, stripes, dazzle, tall grass, horizon',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a zebra, and this is its home — the grassland."\n2) Describe the openness: look how far the grass goes and how big the sky is. Out here there are no walls to hide behind — the zebra needs another trick.\n3) Do the dazzle demo (below): when many zebras stand together, their stripes run together like one wavy picture, and a hunter cannot tell where one zebra stops.\n4) Tell the name-secret: no two zebras wear the same stripes — a zebra knows its mummy in the herd by her pattern alone.\n5) Finish with herd stance: everyone huddles shoulder to shoulder, stripes together, faces out. That is the grassland trick: one pattern, many zebras.',
  say:'"The zebra lives on the grassland, where the grass grows tall and the sky goes on forever. There is nowhere to hide out here — so the zebras hide in each other! When they stand together, their stripes blur into one dazzle and nobody can tell where one zebra ends."',
  notice:'On this card a black-and-white striped zebra with a dark muzzle stands among tall green blades of grass that rise higher than its knees, under a soft pale sky. The word "grassland" sits below — one zebra, in grass, wearing a pattern as bold as its home is open.',
  ask:'"What does the grassland look like — full of trees or full of grass?" "Why do zebras stand together in a herd?" "How does a zebra know its mummy?" "What are the stripes doing when the herd runs?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It lives in the tall grass with the big sky," "The stripes dazzle the hunters!" or the identity line: "Every zebra has different stripes — like names!"',
  real:'Zebra-striped things are everywhere once you look: crossings, rugs, jerseys. At every one, ask the dazzle question — "could you count the zebras in that pattern?" — and watch the idea travel from card to street. A real-optical moment: hold a striped bag against other patterns and see which eye wins.',
  play:'Stripe wave: three or more players (or one child plus soft toys) each hold a striped thing — sock, ribbon, scarf — and shuffle side to side in a line. The counter, ten steps away, must say exactly how many stripes-owners there are. Moving stripes, miscounted. That is the herd, played.',
  tips:'If your child asks why lions do not just watch one zebra, give the honest version: sometimes they manage — but the dazzle makes every bite of watching harder, and out on the grassland, every extra second of confusion is a zebra saved. Nature\u2019s tricks raise the odds; they do not promise miracles.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the zebra live? What is its trick for staying safe? Do two zebras share stripes?" Grassland, herd dazzle, never — three answers and the lesson stands out plain as stripes.'},
 'kangaroo-grassland':{
  title:'The Kangaroo',one:'Built for the bounce — wide-open plains, giant hind legs and an animal that turned hopping into high-speed travel.',
  seoTitle:'Kangaroo Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Grasslands, Hopping and Pouches (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what makes the grassland home suit a kangaroo, why hopping beats walking, how the pouch works, and a hop-race game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the grassland from the kangaroo\u2019s side: open, treeless miles where walking is too slow — so the kangaroo hops, carries its baby in a pouch and owns the bounce.',
  what:'The Australian grassland is wide open tussock country — clumps of coarse grass, scattered shrubs, endless sky. No forests to duck into, no corners to hide behind: a landscape of distance.',
  mean:'In a home made of distance, slow feet go hungry. The kangaroo\u2019s giant hind legs and springy tail make hopping cheaper the faster it goes — a living pogo that crosses the plains in great bounds. And the joey travels inside mum\u2019s pouch: a moving bedroom on a bouncing mum, the safest seat on the grassland.',
  know:'Your child needs three kangaroo ideas: kangaroo country is open grass with far horizons; kangaroos hop — big feet and a balancing tail — and cannot walk backwards at all; and a joey baby rides in its mother\u2019s pouch, drinking milk and peeking out.',
  why:'The kangaroo answers a question every habitat asks — how do you move through THIS home? — with the funniest, bounciest answer in the set. It also sneaks in real physics: hopping is energy-saving at speed, backwards is impossible, and tails are balances, not extras.',
  dev:'Movement science (hop versus walk), energy ideas, body-part function (tail as prop and balance), and care concepts — the pouch as a first nursery, mother as transport, baby as passenger.',
  vocab:'kangaroo, hop, pouch, joey, tail, bound, tussock',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a kangaroo, and this is its home — the open grassland."\n2) Point at the legs: those feet are as long as the kangaroo\u2019s arms — springs, built for bouncing across the miles.\n3) Reveal the two best facts in order: a kangaroo cannot walk backwards — try it yourself, the hop only goes forward — and the tail is a third leg for standing and steering.\n4) Tell the pouch story: a joey rides in mum\u2019s pouch, popping its head out to watch the grass go by. A bedroom that hops.\n5) Hold the hop races (below) and close with the shape of the home: open country, long views, one bouncy specialist.',
  say:'"The kangaroo lives on the open grassland. Its big feet are springs and its tail is a balance — it hops everywhere, faster and faster, and never walks backwards. Baby kangaroos ride along in the pouch, peeking out at the world."',
  notice:'On this card a tan kangaroo with tall ears, a cream belly and big long feet stands upright between clumps of spiky grass, tail resting behind it on the ground. The word "grassland" sits below — one kangaroo, standing tall in a home with nothing but grass and sky.',
  ask:'"How does a kangaroo move — walking or hopping?" "What are the big feet and the tail for?" "Can it walk backwards?" "Where does a joey ride?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It hops with its big springy feet," "Kangaroos can\u2019t go backwards!" or the pouch line: "The baby rides in mum\u2019s pouch and peeks out!"',
  real:'Two hops of kangaroo physics hide in daily life: a ball bounces higher the harder it is thrown (bigger hops cost less per metre), and stairs without a banister feel like open grassland — long views, no handrails, balance doing the work. Name both when you meet them.',
  play:'Hop-course: tape a start line and lay three "tussock" cushions across the room. Rules of the plains: hopping only, feet together, hands up as little paws — touch a cushion and it is a forward bound over it, never a step. Backwards is out of bounds — kangaroos cannot, and neither can the racers. Fastest joey to the far grass wins a peek-from-the-pouch rest.',
  tips:'The cannot-hop-backwards fact is the retell-forever fact — children verify it on their own bodies all week, and every failed backwards attempt re-teaches the lesson. If hopping indoors is too bouncy, ten slow-motion bounds with silent landings is the polite-kangaroo version.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does a kangaroo live? How does it move and why? Where does the joey ride?" Grassland, hops on springy feet, in the pouch — three answers and the race is won.'},
 'elephant-savanna':{
  title:'The Elephant',one:'The gardener of the grass — how the savanna\u2019s biggest animal digs water, opens paths and keeps the whole neighbourhood alive.',
  seoTitle:'Elephant Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Savanna Homes and Elephants as Gardeners (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a savanna is, how elephants dig water holes and shape paths, why the herd walks together, and a trunk-game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches what a savanna is and introduces the set\u2019s biggest idea: some animals do not just live in their habitat — they look after it. The elephant digs, clears and paves the savanna for everyone.',
  what:'A savanna is hot open country — tall dry golden grass, scattered flat-topped acacia trees, a wet season and a dry one. It looks empty from far away and is full of life up close.',
  mean:'Through the dry months, water hides underground — and the elephant hears it. Digging with tusks and feet, it opens water holes that zebra, birds and lions drink from too. Its heavy footsteps clear walking paths through the grass that other animals use as roads, and its dung plants seeds along the way. Take away the elephant and the savanna loses its gardener.',
  know:'Your child needs three savanna ideas: the savanna is golden grass with scattered trees, hot and open; elephants dig water holes that everyone drinks from in the dry season; and elephants live in family herds led by the oldest female — the matriarch, who remembers where the water is.',
  why:'The elephant carries the class\u2019s deepest idea — habitat shapers — in the friendliest wrapper. Children who accept "the elephant helps everyone" have met ecology\u2019s keystone concept years early, through an animal they already adore.',
  dev:'Ecosystem thinking (one animal\u2019s work serving many), memory and leadership respect (the matriarch), trunk coordination, and scale play — the savanna\u2019s distances and the elephant\u2019s size both beg to be paced out.',
  vocab:'elephant, savanna, herd, water hole, trunk, matriarch, acacia',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is an elephant, and this is its home — the savanna."\n2) Read the scene: golden grass, one shady acacia, big grey resident in the middle. Hot country with a wet season and a dry one.\n3) Tell the digging story: in the dry months water hides underground. The elephant hears it, digs with its tusks — and opens a water hole the whole neighbourhood drinks from.\n4) Meet the herd: elephants walk in families, and the boss is the oldest grandmother — she remembers, year after year, exactly where the water waits.\n5) Play the trunk games (below) and close with the title: the savanna\u2019s gardener, on duty.',
  say:'"The elephant lives on the savanna. When the rain stays away, it digs water holes with its tusks — and the zebras, the birds and everyone else come to drink from the elephant\u2019s well. The oldest grandmother leads the family there. She remembers the way."',
  notice:'On this card a large grey elephant with pale tusks, wide ears and a curled trunk stands in golden grass beneath a flat-topped acacia tree, a dark green bush at its feet. The word "savanna" sits below — the biggest gardener, standing in the garden it keeps.',
  ask:'"What does the elephant dig with?" "Who drinks from the water holes it makes?" "Who leads the elephant family?" "What colour is the savanna grass?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It digs wells with its tusks," "Everyone drinks the elephant\u2019s water!" or the leader line: "The oldest grandma knows where the water is."',
  real:'Water-hole thinking works at any park tap or bird bath: "who else drinks here?" The savanna idea — one provider, many guests — maps straight onto the family kitchen too, and the matriarch\u2019s job maps onto whoever in the family remembers where everything is kept.',
  play:'Trunk drills: move a sock across the room using only one arm as an elephant trunk — sweeping, curling and lifting, no hands allowed. Then the water-hole dig: bury a blue cloth in the sandpit and dig one-armed until the "water" shows, trumpet loudly, and lead the toy animals over in a line, oldest toy first. Gardener\u2019s duties, played in full.',
  tips:'Do not skip the grandmother — leadership-by-memory is the fact children quote back most, and it dignifies every older person they know. If your child asks whether the elephant minds sharing its water: that is the whole point of the lesson — on the savanna, the digger digs for everybody.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the elephant live? What does it dig, and who drinks it? Who leads the herd?" Savanna, water holes for everyone, the oldest grandmother — three answers and the well is open.'},
 'lion-savanna':{
  title:'The Lion',one:'Shade, patience and one big roar — the savanna\u2019s famous hunter spends most of its day doing something surprising: napping.',
  seoTitle:'Lion Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Savanna Shade, Pride Resting and Prowling (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how lions live on the savanna, why they rest in the shade by day, how prides work, and a prowl-then-nap game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson corrects the biggest lion misconception before it settles: the king of the savanna is a champion rester. Hot hours belong to the shade, the pride naps together — and the prowling comes later, when the air cools.',
  what:'The savanna heats up hard in the middle of the day. Shade under an acacia, a warm patch of sand and a pile of family is what a lion\u2019s daytime looks like: rest, doze, groom, repeat — with tufts of tall grass and green bush nearby.',
  mean:'Hunting is expensive and midday heat is brutal — so the lion\u2019s plan is timing. Sleep through the hot hours on a full stomach\u2019s worth of patience; hunt at dusk and dawn when the air cools and the prey\u2019s eyes struggle. The tawny coat matches the dry grass exactly, and the pride — the lion family — shares the shade, the cubs and the hunt.',
  know:'Your child needs three lion ideas: lions rest up to twenty hours a day, usually together in the shade; a lion family is called a pride and the lionesses do most of the hunting; and a lion\u2019s tawny coat disappears against dry grass — camouflage it wears like a uniform.',
  why:'The lion flips the loudest story children know (fierce, always chasing) into a truer one (patient, rested, tactical). That correction is a thinking lesson in itself: the real king of the savanna wins by resting well and timing well — not by roaring all day.',
  dev:'Energy and rest concepts (why hunters save their strength), time-of-day thinking (cool hours versus hot hours), family-structure vocabulary (pride, lioness, cub), and color-matching reasoning — coat against grass, disguise against background.',
  vocab:'lion, pride, mane, shade, prowl, tawny, roar',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a lion, and this is its home — the savanna."\n2) Flip the story gently: the lion looks fierce — and it is — but its favourite thing is a very long nap in the shade. Up to twenty hours a day.\n3) Ask why: hunting is hard work, midday is hot, and big runners overheat. So the pride rests together and hunts in the cool of morning and evening.\n4) Point at the coat: golden-tawny, exactly the colour of dry grass. Standing still, a lion half vanishes — the grass hides it.\n5) Meet the pride: lionesses hunt together; cubs tumble in the shade; the big dark mane says "grown-up". Finish by playing prowl-then-nap (below).',
  say:'"The lion lives on the savanna and rests in the shade nearly all day — the pride naps together, cubs and all. When the air turns cool, the lionesses rise, stretch and prowl through the tall grass, quiet as shadows, their coats the very colour of home."',
  notice:'On this card a golden lion with a full dark-brown mane and a calm, friendly face sits upright on warm sand, green shrubs and pale sky behind it, tail curled at its side. The word "savanna" sits below — the king at rest, between patrols.',
  ask:'"What is the lion doing most of the day?" "Why does it rest in the shade?" "What is a lion family called?" "Which lions do most of the hunting?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It naps in the shade — twenty hours!" "The daddy lion has the big dark mane," or the prowl line: "The lionesses hunt together when it gets cool."',
  real:'Shade mathematics is everywhere: on a hot day, measure the cool with your child — under the tree versus on the path, one hand each. The lion\u2019s midday plan is a park-bench plan. And at dusk, point out when the birds and bats swap shifts: the cool hours belong to the prowlers.',
  play:'Prowl-then-nap: everyone lies in a shade pile for a slow count of twenty — proper pride napping, shoulders touching. Then the lionesses rise, stretch long, and prowl one silent circle of the room, feet soft, bodies low. Only after the prowl comes the roar — one big family roar, then straight back to the nap. Energy saved, timing learned.',
  tips:'Keep the mane clear without fuss: it is a grown-up boy-lion feature, the way a beard is. And if your child is disappointed the lion naps so much, lean in — that is the twist the whole lesson hangs on: the mightiest cat on the savanna wins by being still. Rest is not the opposite of fierce; it is what makes it possible.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the lion spend the hot hours? What is its family called? What colour is its coat, and what does it match?" In the shade, a pride, tawny like the grass — three answers and the pride sleeps on.'},
 'camel-desert':{
  title:'The Camel',one:'The desert\u2019s own design — wide feet, long lashes and a hump full of travel fuel, built for the driest home on Earth.',
  seoTitle:'Camel Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Desert Homes and How a Camel Beats the Heat (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a desert is, how a camel\u2019s hump, feet and lashes beat sand and sun, and a dune-walking balance game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the desert as a real home — hot, dry, wide and full of sand — and walks through the camel\u2019s equipment piece by piece, starting with the biggest mix-up of all: what the hump actually holds.',
  what:'A desert is a land of very little rain — rolling dunes, bare rock, blazing days and chilly nights. Few plants grow, water hides in rare wells and oases, and the sand itself shifts with the wind.',
  mean:'Every desert problem has a camel part as its answer. Scorching sun: long eyelashes and closable nostrils keep the sand out. Burning ground: wide padded feet that spread on soft sand like snowshoes. Long empty miles between wells: a hump that stores fat — not water! — to burn as travel fuel. The camel is not just in the desert; the desert is written all over it.',
  know:'Your child needs three desert ideas: deserts are the driest lands on Earth, all sand, rock and rare water; the camel\u2019s hump stores fat for the long journey — not water; and its feet, lashes and nostrils are each a piece of desert equipment.',
  why:'The camel delivers body-part reasoning at its clearest: hold up a problem (sun, sand, distance), name the part that solves it. Children learn to read an animal like a toolbox — a habit of thinking that transfers to every animal they will ever meet.',
  dev:'Problem-and-solution matching, sun-safety parallels (lashes like sunglasses, shade-seeking), balance skills through dune-walking games, and myth-busting confidence — your child now knows something most grown-ups get wrong.',
  vocab:'camel, desert, hump, dune, oasis, lashes, padded feet',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a camel, and this is its home — the desert."\n2) Fix the mix-up first, proudly: everyone says the hump holds water. It does not — it holds FAT, packed fuel for the long walk between wells.\n3) Run the equipment check: long lashes and shut-tight nostrils against the sand; wide spongy feet that grip soft dunes; knees built to kneel on hot ground.\n4) Explain the distance: in the desert, wells and oases sit far apart — the camel is the animal that crosses the gap and arrives ready for more.\n5) Play the dune walk (below) and close with the summary: sun, sand, distance — a part for every problem.',
  say:'"The camel lives in the desert, where the sun is hot and the sand goes on and on. Its hump is full of fat, not water — food for the long walk. Its lashes keep the sand out, its wide feet stay on top of the dunes, and on it goes, mile after mile."',
  notice:'On this card a sandy-tan camel with one neat hump, a short mane and a gentle face stands on firm sand, rolling dunes rising behind it under a pale clear sky. The word "desert" sits below — one traveller, at home between the dunes.',
  ask:'"What does the hump really store?" "Why does the desert not bother the camel\u2019s eyes?" "Why are its feet so wide?" "What do we call a green, watery spot in the desert?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "The hump is fat, not water — for the long walk!" "Its lashes stop the sand," or the feet line: "Wide feet so it doesn\u2019t sink in the sand!"',
  real:'Sun-gear comparison works anywhere bright: sunglasses as lashes, hat as shade-seek, water bottle as oasis plan. On sand at a beach or sandpit, test the feet rule yourself — flat hands or shoes sink less than heels. The camel\u2019s engineering is kid-testable.',
  play:'Dune walk: lay a line of cushions or a strip of tape as a "dune ridge". Camel rules: heel-to-toe, arms out, eyes up — wobble off and it is back to the start oasis. Round two carries cargo (a beanbag on the head — the hump). Steady, wide, unhurried: the camel gait is the winning gait.',
  tips:'Let your child correct YOU on the hump at least once — act the mistake ("the hump is full of water, right?") and enjoy the triumphant "NO — FAT!". Being the family expert on one fact is a memory glue no drill can match.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the hump store? Name two camel parts that beat the desert. Where does a camel walk between?" Fat, lashes-and-wide-feet, well to well — three answers and the caravan moves on.',
  safety:'Desert talk is sun-safety talk: hats, water and shade are the human version of lashes and humps. On real hot days, drink before thirsty and never play unattended near water — even the oasis has rules.'},
 'beaver-river':{
  title:'The Beaver',one:'The builder that rebuilds its river — sticks, mud and engineering that turn flowing water into a private pond.',
  seoTitle:'Beaver Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching River Homes, Dams and Animal Builders (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a river is, how beavers build dams and lodges, why their ponds help other animals, and a build-a-dam game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson introduces the one animal in the class that constructs its own habitat: the beaver fells sticks, dams the stream, and pools the river into a pond deep enough to hide a front door under water.',
  what:'A river is moving fresh water — running from hills to sea, edged with reeds, willows and stones. It flows, it ripples and it changes its banks with every season.',
  mean:'Moving water is a poor bedroom — so the beaver changes the speed. It drags branches and stones into the stream, packs the gaps with mud, and the backed-up water spreads into a still pond. In the middle it builds its lodge: a dome of sticks with the entrance UNDER the water, safe from hungry visitors. One busy builder, and the running river becomes a quiet, deep, private home.',
  know:'Your child needs three beaver ideas: rivers are running fresh water with grassy banks; beavers build dams from sticks and mud that turn the flow into a pond; and they live in a lodge with an underwater front door, reached by diving.',
  why:'The beaver is engineering made visible at bath-toy scale: block the flow, still the water, build the house. It is also the set\u2019s bridge between habitats — the beaver\u2019s pond becomes frog-and-fish housing, planting the idea that one builder\u2019s work opens homes for many.',
  dev:'Construction play and sequencing (gather, stack, pack), cause-and-effect with real water, tool reasoning (teeth as axes, tails as rudders), and generosity concepts — building that benefits the whole neighbourhood.',
  vocab:'beaver, river, dam, lodge, pond, paddle tail, sticks',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a beaver, and this is its home — the riverbank."\n2) State the problem like a builder: rivers RUN — too fast for a quiet house. So the beaver does something no other animal here does: it rebuilds the river.\n3) Walk the build: drag sticks and stones into the water, pack the gaps with mud — the water backs up and spreads into a still pond. That wall is called a dam.\n4) Tour the lodge: a dome of sticks in the pond, and the front door is UNDER the water — you get home by diving.\n5) Build a mini dam (below) and close with the generosity: the new pond becomes a home for frogs, fish and birds too.',
  say:'"The beaver lives by the river — but first it fixes the river. It piles up sticks and mud until the water stops and makes a pond, then builds a round stick house with its door hidden under the water. Dive in, pop up inside: home. And the new pond welcomes everyone."',
  notice:'On this card a chestnut-brown beaver with a cream muzzle, two big front teeth and a flat paddle tail sits on a grassy bank beside blue water, reeds and bushes at the edges. The word "river" sits below — the builder on its own riverfront, sticks nearby.',
  ask:'"Why does a beaver build a dam?" "What is the dam made of?" "Where is the lodge\u2019s door?" "Who else comes to live in the beaver\u2019s pond?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It blocks the river with sticks and mud," "Its door is under the water — it dives home!" or the neighbour line: "The pond is for the frogs and fish too!"',
  real:'Any flowing water is a beaver lecture: watch the current push a leaf and ask "could you build a house in water this fast? What would you do first?" Concrete tip: look for gnawed sticks and stumps with tooth-scarves — beaver country signs, in real parks.',
  play:'Bath or basin dam: tilt a slow trickle from the tap or a jug, then let your child stop it with a dam of twigs, duplo and foil, watching the water pool behind. Then the flood test — remove one stick and watch the leak teach engineering faster than any explanation. Dry-run version: build the dam with blocks across a hallway "river" of blue cloth.',
  tips:'Say "engineer" out loud and often — a four-year-old who calls the beaver an animal engineer owns a word most meet at school age. And when the dam leaks, celebrate: leaks are how real dams teach. The beaver patches with mud; your child patches with one more block.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a beaver build first? What is the wall called? Where is the front door of the lodge?" A dam, sticks-and-mud, under the water — three answers and the pond is filling.',
  safety:'Real rivers and ponds earn the standing rule: grown-up hand within arm\u2019s reach at any natural water, no leaning over banks, and hands washed after water play. The beaver dives safely; small people stay on the bank.'},
 'turtle-coast':{
  title:'The Turtle',one:'Two homes in one address — the sea for swimming, the beach for resting, and a sand-nursery for the tiniest turtles.',
  seoTitle:'Turtle Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Coastal Homes, Sea Turtles and Beach Nurseries (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the coast is, how sea turtles live between beach and sea, where baby turtles hatch, and a two-homes dash game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the coast as the meeting of two homes — land\u2019s sand and sea\u2019s waves — through the turtle, an animal that keeps one flipper in each: swimming all day, basking ashore, and hatching its babies right on the beach.',
  what:'The coast is where the sea meets the land: sandy beaches, wet sand at the wave-line, dunes behind, shallows ahead. It belongs to neither world and both at once.',
  mean:'The turtle uses both halves of the coast. The sea feeds it — seagrass and jellyfish in the waves. The beach serves it — warm sand for basking, and, one night in a mother turtle\u2019s life, the nursery: eggs buried in a sand nest above the tide-line, where the sun keeps them warm until the tiniest turtles dig out and dash for the waves.',
  know:'Your child needs three coast ideas: the coast is where beach meets sea — two habitats touching; sea turtles swim in the surf but rest on the sand; and baby turtles hatch from eggs buried in the dunes and walk to the water on their very first night.',
  why:'The turtle holds the set\u2019s first two-address life, and the baby-dash story is nature\u2019s greatest cliffhanger — a journey every child can retell with their whole body. Coasts are also where most families actually meet the sea, so this card converts holidays into habitat lessons.',
  dev:'Two-category thinking (land home and water home in one life), sequencing the hatchling story, wave-and-sand sensory vocabulary, and empathy with fragility — the tiniest turtle against the gulls makes care feel urgent.',
  vocab:'turtle, coast, beach, wave, sand, nest, hatchling',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a sea turtle, and this is its home — the coast."\n2) Split the scene: one side sand, one side sea. Ask which half the turtle uses — and reveal the answer: both. Swim in the sea, rest on the sand.\n3) Tell the nursery story: a mother turtle hauls up at night, digs a nest above the waves with her back flippers, and buries her eggs in the warm sand.\n4) Tell the dash: weeks later the eggs hatch, and the tiniest turtles ever dig out and walk, wobble and sprint to the waves — their first journey, the night they are born.\n5) Play the hatchling dash (below) and close with the address: sea for lunch, beach for bed.',
  say:'"The sea turtle lives at the coast, where the sand meets the sea. It swims among the waves all day and rests on the warm sand. And do you know where baby turtles are born? In a nest in the sand — then they dash to the sea, the very night they hatch!"',
  notice:'On this card a green sea turtle with a patterned shell, scaly flippers and a smiling face rests on pale wet sand at the exact line where a gentle blue wave curls up the beach. The word "coast" sits below — half sand, half sea, both home.',
  ask:'"What are the two parts of the coast?" "What does the turtle do in the sea? On the sand?" "Where are turtle eggs laid?" "What do the babies do the night they hatch?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It swims in the sea and sleeps on the beach," "The eggs are in a sand nest," or the dash line: "The babies run to the water as fast as they can, the first night!"',
  real:'Beach holidays become habitat audits with one question: which half is this — turtle sand or turtle sea? Wave-line footprints, tide marks and dune grass all feed the lesson, and any lifeguard-flag chat doubles as the safety briefing below.',
  play:'Hatchling dash: mark a "nest" line at one end of the room and a "sea" of blue cloth at the other. Hatchlings crouch low in the nest, count to three, then make the run — wobbly flippers on the arms, fastest dash allowed. A "gull" player swoops a scarf overhead to make the run honest. Reach the sea and you may swim in circles, shouting the good news.',
  tips:'Keep the gull playful, never scary — the dash is a triumph story, and most hatchlings make it. If your child asks how the babies know where the sea is: the horizon glows brighter over the water, and the waves whisper. True, and just magical enough to retell.',
  check:'Ask: "What two homes meet at the coast? Where does the mother lay her eggs? What do the babies do?" Sand-and-sea, a sand nest, dash to the waves — three answers and the lesson crawls safely home.',
  safety:'Beach rules are turtle rules: swim between the flags with a grown-up, never turn your back on the waves, and if you ever find a real nest, look with eyes only — the sand nursery must not be touched.'},
 'fish-ocean':{
  title:'The Fish',one:'Breathing water itself — the ocean as the planet\u2019s biggest home, and the gills that let a fish live in it for free.',
  seoTitle:'Fish Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Ocean Habitats and How Gills Work (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how big the ocean is, how fish breathe water through their gills, what fins and tails do, and a gill-breathing game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the ocean — the biggest habitat on the planet — with its most numerous resident. The fish teaches the swap nobody can make: we breathe air, fish breathe water, and gills are the reason.',
  what:'The ocean is one enormous connected sea covering most of the Earth — deeper than mountains are tall, from sunlit shallows to the sandy floors. All of it is one address: every drop touches every other drop.',
  mean:'Land animals carry their air around inside them; fish skip the carrying. Water pours in the mouth, washes over the gills on each side of the head — feather-red filters that grab the oxygen — and out it flows. Fins steer, the tail drives, and the whole body is shaped to slide through a home that has no floor you could trip on and no ceiling you could bump.',
  know:'Your child needs three ocean ideas: the ocean is the biggest habitat on Earth, bigger than all the land homes together; fish breathe the water itself through their gills — they would suffocate in our air; and fins and tails are their steering and engine.',
  why:'Gills are the cleanest possible contrast lesson: same need, opposite solution. Once a child can say "we could never swap — I breathe air, the fish breathes water," they hold the deepest fact about habitats: the home and the breather must match.',
  dev:'Respiration basics in child words, scale thinking (biggest home on the planet), body-part function (gills, fins, tail), and humble awe — the ocean is too big to visit all of, and that bigness is the lesson.',
  vocab:'fish, ocean, gills, fin, tail, water, scale',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a fish, and this is its home — the ocean."\n2) Size it honestly: the ocean covers most of the globe — spin a globe or map and let the blue win. Biggest home, no contest.\n3) Do the gill story: behind each fish cheek sits a red, feathery filter. Water in the mouth, past the gills — out the sides. The fish is breathing the water itself.\n4) Try the swap-proof: pinch your nose and mouth gently for one second — we cannot breathe water; the fish cannot breathe air. Never swap. That is why the ocean is its home and ours is ours.\n5) Finish with the fins: steering wheels and engine. Then play gill-breathing (below).',
  say:'"The fish lives in the ocean, the biggest home on the whole Earth. It does not breathe air like us — it breathes the water! The water runs in its mouth, through its red gills and out again. Its tail pushes and its fins steer. We could never swap homes."',
  notice:'On this card a rounded blue fish with a pale belly, dark fins and a big friendly eye swims above a sandy seabed with a soft wave of water behind it. The word "ocean" sits below — one fish, gliding through the biggest room in the world.',
  ask:'"What does a fish breathe — air or water?" "Where are the gills?" "What do the tail and fins do?" "Which is bigger, the ocean or all the land?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It breathes water through its gills," "The ocean is the biggest home on Earth!" or the swap line: "We couldn\u2019t live there and it couldn\u2019t live here — we breathe different things!"',
  real:'Aquarium walls and fish counters are gill theatres: watch the mouth open-shut rhythm and name it — "that is breathing, water edition". A globe\u2019s blue share is the size lesson, repeatable every time the world map comes out.',
  play:'Gill pantomime: cheeks puffed, mouth open-shut in slow fish-breath, glide across the room with palms pressed together as the tail. Mid-game call the swap: "SWAP!" — everyone must gasp one big human breath and shout what they breathe. Air and water, two breaths, one game.',
  tips:'Keep gills concrete: finger-draw the two cheek lines on your own face and your child\u2019s — "gills here, filters here". If asked whether the ocean ever ends, give the honest size: from space the Earth is called the blue planet because the fish\u2019s home is bigger than all of ours together.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the biggest habitat on Earth? What do fish breathe, and with what?" Ocean, water-through-gills — two giant answers and the lesson is deep enough.'},
 'dolphin-ocean':{
  title:'The Dolphin',one:'The breather that leaps — an ocean neighbour with lungs like ours, a whistle like a name and a jump that is really a breath.',
  seoTitle:'Dolphin Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Ocean Air-Breathers and Dolphin Talk (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why dolphins leap to breathe, how their clicks and whistles work, what a pod is, and a surface-for-breath game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson adds the ocean\u2019s great twist: not everyone under the waves breathes water. The dolphin holds its breath like us, leaps to refill — and greets its family with a whistle that works like a name.',
  what:'Dolphins live in the open ocean and along the coasts, roaming in pods — chattering family groups that hunt fish, surf waves and leap in arcs that show their pale bellies to the sky.',
  mean:'A dolphin is a mammal in a fish\u2019s world: lungs, not gills. So life in the ocean is a rhythm — swim, swim, surface, BREATH, dive again. The leap is not showing off; it is breathing taken at speed. And each dolphin carries its own signature whistle — a sound it makes so others can call it, the closest thing in nature to having a name.',
  know:'Your child needs three dolphin ideas: dolphins breathe AIR with lungs, like us — they hold their breath underwater and leap up to refill; they talk in clicks and whistles, each dolphin with its own signature sound; and they swim in pods — families that stay together.',
  why:'The dolphin completes the fish lesson: same ocean, two breathing plans — gills below, lungs leaping above. The signature whistle also gives children their first "animals have names for each other" moment, which is empathy-building of the strongest kind.',
  dev:'Breath-hold awareness (a real, feelable skill), mammal-fish contrast reasoning, sound-and-name concepts, and cooperative play — pods take turns, pods stay together, and so do good games.',
  vocab:'dolphin, breathe, leap, blowhole, whistle, pod, dive',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a dolphin, and this is its home — the ocean."\n2) Spring the twist: it lives in the sea but does NOT breathe water. Lungs, like yours! It holds its breath below and leaps up — whoosh — to breathe again.\n3) Find the blowhole: on top of the head, a little trapdoor for air. One jump can be one giant breath.\n4) Do dolphin talk: clicks and whistles. Every dolphin has its own signature whistle — call it, and it answers. A name made of sound.\n5) Play surface-and-breath (below), then close with the pod: dolphins travel together, eat together, breathe together.',
  say:'"The dolphin lives in the ocean but breathes air, just like us. It swims, and swims, and then leaps up — hoo! — one big breath through the blowhole on its head, and down again. Its family calls it with its very own whistle."',
  notice:'On this card a sleek grey dolphin with a pale belly, a friendly eye and a small beak-like smile arcs above rolling blue waves, its curved back and tail fin clear of the water. The word "ocean" sits below — mid-leap, mid-breath, exactly where a dolphin lives its best moment.',
  ask:'"Does a dolphin breathe water or air?" "Where is the blowhole?" "Why does it leap out of the water?" "What is a dolphin\u2019s whistle like?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It breathes air — it jumps up for a big breath!" "The hole is on top of its head," or the name line: "Every dolphin has its own whistle, like a name!"',
  real:'Breath rhythm is testable in the bath: how long can your child hold a comfortable breath? (Three to five seconds, cheerfully.) That number IS the dolphin\u2019s problem, felt in miniature. At any aquarium or beach, the surface-leap watch becomes a breath count.',
  play:'Pod relay: crawl-swim across the carpet, then "surface" at set points — touch the wall, stand, one huge breath with arms up as the blowhole opens, back down and glide on. Rule of the pod: nobody surfaces alone — the whole family leaps together, breathes together and giggles together.',
  tips:'Give each family member a signature whistle on the spot — a two-note hum each — and use them for a day of calling across rooms. The dolphin\u2019s naming idea converts into a family game with zero props, and every call retells the lesson.',
  check:'Ask: "Does a dolphin breathe water or air? What is the hole on its head called? What is its whistle for?" Air, blowhole, calling its name — three answers and the pod leaps on.'},
 'whale-ocean':{
  title:'The Whale',one:'The biggest animal that ever lived — a gentle giant of the open ocean that sings, breathes at the surface and eats the sea\u2019s tiniest meals.',
  seoTitle:'Whale Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching the Ocean\u2019s Biggest Animal and Whale Songs (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how big a whale really is, how it breathes and sings, what it eats, and a whale-size measuring game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson ends the ocean trio with scale: the whale is the largest animal that has ever lived on Earth — bigger than any dinosaur find — and it got that way by eating some of the smallest meals in the sea.',
  what:'Whales roam the whole ocean, from polar feeding seas to warm nursery bays. The biggest — blue whales — grow longer than three cars in a row, with hearts the size of a small armchair.',
  mean:'Size like that runs on surprises. A whale breathes air at the surface through its blowhole — one mighty breath lasting minutes below. It sings — long, booming songs that travel whole seas, whale-to-whale. And the blue whale\u2019s diet is among the tiniest: it gulps enormous mouthfuls of water and strains out clouds of shrimp-like krill, millions at a time. Biggest animal, tiniest meals, gentlest table manners.',
  know:'Your child needs three whale ideas: whales are the biggest animals that have ever lived — bigger than the dinosaurs we dig up; they breathe air at the surface through a blowhole, like the dolphin; and the biggest whales eat tiny krill by the million, strained from great gulps of sea.',
  why:'Whales hand children their first true "wow beyond belief" fact that is also verifiable — and verifying it (pace it out, lie in it, fit the family inside) teaches the habit of checking wonder against reality. Big-and-gentle also stretches character thinking: the ocean\u2019s giant is no one\u2019s monster.',
  dev:'Scale measurement (pacing, comparing, lying inside the size), singing and sound-travel curiosity, food-chain inversion (biggest eats smallest), and calm confidence — giants are not scary when you know their manners.',
  vocab:'whale, blowhole, krill, giant, song, gulp, baleen',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a whale, and this is its home — the whole wide ocean."\n2) Land the size slowly: the biggest animal that has EVER lived. Bigger than every dinosaur bone in every museum. Longer than a bus — often three.\n3) Breathe like a whale: one surface breath through the blowhole, then minutes below. (Same plan as the dolphin — the ocean has two breathing teams.)\n4) Sing the secret: whales sing songs that boom across whole seas to family far away. Deep water carries voices like nothing on land.\n5) Serve the surprise meal: the giant eats TINY food — a mouthful of sea, strained through baleen, millions of krill kept, water back out. Then pace the whale out, floor to hallway (below).',
  say:'"The whale is the biggest animal that has ever lived — bigger than a bus, bigger than the dinosaurs! It breathes at the top through its blowhole, sings songs that travel across the whole sea, and eats tiny little krill by the million. The biggest giant in the world is a gentle one."',
  notice:'On this card a deep-blue whale with a pale, ridged belly and a small kind eye glides above rolling waves, its long body and big tail fin filling the picture. The word "ocean" sits below — the giant itself, at ease in the only home big enough.',
  ask:'"Is the whale bigger or smaller than a bus?" "How does it breathe — gills or a blowhole?" "What does the biggest whale eat?" "Why does it sing?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It\u2019s the biggest animal EVER — bigger than dinosaurs!" "It breathes through its blowhole at the top," or the surprise line: "It eats tiny krill — millions of them, in one gulp!"',
  real:'Whale scale is floor-space fun: three parked cars nose-to-tail, one long school corridor, a whole swimming lane plus a bit. Museums with whale hangs deliver the wow vertically. Sound-travel talk works anywhere loud: cup your ears — in the sea, a whale\u2019s song would beat any speaker.',
  play:'Whale-length: heel-toe the family down the hallway or pavement until you reach whale length (three car-lengths, or about twenty of your child\u2019s steps). Lie down in your whale, nose to toes, arms out — the whole family fits inside one whale with room to spare. Then one family blowhole-breath, together, and a hummed two-note whale-song to send it swimming.',
  tips:'Let the size fact stay enormous — do not sand its edges with "about as big as". Children this age treasure one impossible-but-true fact above all toys; "the biggest animal ever is alive right now" is the one worth lending them. If asked what whales say, hum low and slow: whale songs are felt in the chest before the ears.',
  check:'Ask: "Is the whale the biggest animal ever? How does it breathe? What does it eat?" Yes — ever, blowhole air, tiny krill by the million — three answers and the giant swims on.'},
 'penguin-polar-region':{
  title:'The Penguin',one:'A bird built for the cold — feathers like a down coat, wings that swim, and a huddle that shares the warmth fairly.',
  seoTitle:'Penguin Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Polar Homes, Huddles and Feather Coats (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the polar region is, how penguin feathers beat the cold, why they huddle, and a waddle-slide-huddle game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson travels to the white end of the Earth: the polar region, where the ground itself is ice. The penguin shows how a bird with no fur, no den and no trees still keeps warm — feathers, blubber, and each other.',
  what:'The polar regions are the freezing ends of the Earth — sea ice, ice shelves and water that would numb a finger in seconds. No trees grow, few land plants — the "ground" is white and the sea below is full of fish.',
  mean:'The penguin\u2019s warmth is layered. Outside: dense feathers, oiled and overlapping — a windproof coat. Under the skin: a thick blanket of fat called blubber. And when the wind really bites, penguins do the trick no single bird can: they huddle, hundreds deep, shoulders tucked, and slowly rotate so everyone takes a turn in the warm middle and nobody stays cold at the edge.',
  know:'Your child needs three polar ideas: the polar region is the icy end of the Earth, where the sea itself freezes; penguins are birds that swim with their wings instead of flying; and they keep warm with feather coats, blubber — and the famous huddle.',
  why:'The penguin teaches warmth as cooperation: the huddle only works because everyone shares the cold edge. It also corrects the bird story — wings are not only for flying — and it hands families the fairest warmth game ever invented.',
  dev:'Insulation science at coat level (feathers, blubber, huddling), turn-taking ethics built into the rotation, swimming-versus-flying body reasoning, and cold-weather empathy — the outside-of-the-huddle seat is real.',
  vocab:'penguin, polar, ice, huddle, feathers, blubber, waddle',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a penguin, and this is its home — the polar region, where even the sea turns to ice."\n2) Meet the coat: thousands of tiny feathers, overlapping like roof tiles, plus a fat blanket underneath. The cold cannot get through either easily.\n3) Flip the wings: these are birds that gave up flying in the AIR to fly in the WATER — wings as flippers, faster than they look.\n4) Tell the huddle: when the wind howls, penguins pack together tight and take turns — cold penguins on the outside shuffle slowly to the warm middle, and warm ones take their turn at the edge. Sharing, in feather coats.\n5) Play waddle-slide-huddle (below) and close with the address: ice above, fish below, friends all around.',
  say:'"The penguin lives in the polar region, where the ground is ice and the sea is freezing. Its feather coat and fat blanket keep it warm — and when the wind blows hard, the penguins huddle up tight and take turns in the warm middle. Nobody stays cold alone."',
  notice:'On this card a black-backed, white-bellied penguin with an orange beak and feet stands squarely on a low white ice floe, calm and front-facing, pale sky and sea around it. The words "polar region" sit below — one bird, dressed for the coldest address there is.',
  ask:'"Why doesn\u2019t the penguin freeze?" "What are its wings for?" "What do penguins do when the wind gets too cold?" "Who stands at the edge of the huddle — and what happens next?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It has a feather coat and a fat blanket," "Its wings swim — it flies in the water!" or the huddle line: "They take turns — the cold ones go to the middle and warm ones go to the edge."',
  real:'Winter dressing becomes feather logic: coat = feather layer, jumper = blubber layer, hugging a grown-up = huddle. Ice in a drink shows the floe from below. And any bath-time raft of toys floating on the water is an ice floe waiting for a penguin passenger.',
  play:'Waddle-slide-huddle: waddle across the room, feet out, arms tucked — then belly-slide the final stretch onto a cushion floe. Call "WIND!" and everyone packs into one tight back-huddle, arms wrapped, shoulders touching. Rotate the huddle one slow turn so the edge players move toward the middle — fair shares, warm hearts, zero frostbite.',
  tips:'Honour the turn-taking explicitly: "at the edge, then the middle — everyone gets both." If your child asks whether penguins miss flying, give the swim answer proudly: they traded one sky for another, and theirs is full of fish.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the penguin live? What keeps it warm — three things?" Polar region, feathers-blubber-huddle — one big answer in three parts and the lesson is warm through.',
  safety:'Ice is for penguins: real frozen water is never for walking on, however thick it looks. Cold-weather dressing follows the penguin plan — layers first, hat always — and snowy play ends with dry clothes and warm hands.'},
 'seal-polar-region':{
  title:'The Seal',one:'The ice-and-water life — a blubber-wrapped swimmer that flops out to rest on the floes and slips back to hunt below.',
  seoTitle:'Seal Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Polar Waters, Blubber and Ice Resting (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how seals live between ice and sea, what blubber does, how whiskers hunt in the dark, and a blubber-glove experiment for home.',
  intro:'This lesson stays in the polar region to meet the seal — the neighbour who is half-in and half-out of the water: sleek torpedo below the surface, contented loaf above the ice, wrapped in the sea\u2019s warmest coat.',
  what:'Polar seas freeze at the top into floes and ice shelves while staying liquid below. Seals need BOTH: open water to hunt fish, and solid ice to haul out on — for resting, napping in the sun and raising pups.',
  mean:'The seal\u2019s secret is blubber: a thick layer of fat under the skin that works like a wetsuit the seal never takes off — holding body heat in the water that would freeze us in minutes. On the ice it flops and dozes; below it swims like a bullet, steered by back flippers, hunting by feel: its whiskers are so sensitive they read the water itself, feeling fish move in the dark.',
  know:'Your child needs three seal ideas: seals split their lives between ice (rest) and water (hunting); blubber — a thick fat layer — is their built-in warm coat; and their whiskers can feel fish swimming past in dark water, like fingers that read the sea.',
  why:'The seal delivers the class\u2019s best do-it-yourself science: the blubber-glove experiment is safe, quick and unforgettable. It also rounds the polar picture — penguin huddles on the surface, seal below it — showing one habitat hosting different answers to the same cold.',
  dev:'Insulation experimenting (blubber-glove test), touch-sensitivity concepts (whiskers as feelers), two-world scheduling (ice shifts and water shifts), and gentle body-play — the seal scoot is comedy with core strength in it.',
  vocab:'seal, blubber, floe, haul out, whiskers, flipper, dive',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a seal, and this is its home — the polar sea and its ice."\n2) Split its day: below the water — torpedo, hunting fish; on top of the ice — a happy loaf, dozing in the sun. Both halves are home.\n3) Introduce blubber: under the seal\u2019s skin sits a thick coat of fat called blubber — the reason icy water feels like a cool bath to it. We will test how it works in a minute.\n4) Meet the whiskers: each one is a feeler that reads the water. In the dark under the ice, a seal\u2019s face finds fish by feel alone.\n5) Run the blubber-glove test (below), do the seal scoot, and close with the address: ice to rest, sea to hunt.',
  say:'"The seal lives in the polar region — it rests on the ice and hunts in the sea. Under its skin it wears blubber, a thick warm coat it never takes off. Its whiskers feel the water in the dark, so no fish swims past unnoticed. Half its life is a nap; the other half is a hunt."',
  notice:'On this card a silver-grey seal with dark spots, long whiskers and a gentle face rests on a low white ice floe, snowy peaks rising behind it on the pale sea. The words "polar region" sit below — hauled out, warm inside its coat, between two naps of its two-shift day.',
  ask:'"Where does the seal rest, and where does it hunt?" "What is blubber?" "What can its whiskers feel?" "Why can WE not swim in that water?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It sleeps on the ice and eats fish in the sea," "Blubber is its warm coat — like a wetsuit!" or the whisker boast: "It feels the fish with its whiskers in the dark!"',
  real:'Cold-water feel: one hand in a bowl of cold water bare, the other inside a bag-glove — the temperature gap between protected and bare skin IS the blubber lesson, felt in thirty seconds. Winter coats, wetsuits and duvets are all family blubber once named.',
  play:'Blubber-glove lab: one hand bare, one hand inside a plastic bag lined with thick fat (shortening) or doubled mittens — both into bowls of icy water side by side. Count the seconds until the bare hand begs out while the blubber hand stays cosy. Then the seal scoot: flat on the tummy, push up on palms, flop forward three times across the room — hauling out, at home.',
  tips:'Run the glove test fairly — same bowls, same minute — so the result is the child\u2019s discovery, not your claim. If asked whether blubber is heavy, answer honestly: yes, and that is fine — in water, heavy and floaty are friends. Seals sink to swim; the coat does double duty.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the two halves of a seal\u2019s day? What keeps it warm in freezing water? What do its whiskers do?" Ice-and-sea, blubber, feel the fish — three answers and the floe is occupied.'},
 'polar-bear-arctic':{
  title:'The Polar Bear',one:'White on white at the top of the world — the arctic\u2019s biggest hunter wears its home as camouflage and waits on the ice for its dinner to breathe.',
  seoTitle:'Polar Bear Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Arctic Ice, Camouflage and Patient Hunting (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the arctic is, how polar bear fur vanishes against snow, how it hunts at breathing holes, and a camouflage test for home.',
  intro:'This lesson finishes the cold pair at the very top of the map: the arctic, and the biggest bear on Earth. The polar bear teaches camouflage at its purest — and patience as a hunting tool that works by staying still.',
  what:'The arctic is the frozen ocean at the North Pole: sea ice drifting in slabs and sheets over deep, dark water, months of winter dark, and snow from horizon to horizon.',
  mean:'A white bear on white ice is nearly invisible — and invisibility is the polar bear\u2019s whole hunting plan. Seals must breathe through holes in the ice; the bear finds a hole, sits down, and waits — still as the snow around it — sometimes for hours, until a nose breaks the surface. One swift paw, and the patient hunt ends. The ice is not scenery here; it is the kitchen floor, the road and the dining table at once.',
  know:'Your child needs three arctic ideas: the arctic is the frozen sea at the top of the world, all ice and snow; the polar bear\u2019s creamy coat matches it so well the bear can vanish — camouflage in action; and it hunts by waiting, perfectly still, at the seals\u2019 breathing holes.',
  why:'The polar bear completes the camouflage thread started by the lion (tawny on gold) with its opposite number (white on white) — two hunters, two colours, one trick. And the breathing-hole vigil teaches stillness as strength: the mightiest hunter in the arctic wins by not moving.',
  dev:'Camouflage testing (hide-and-seek science), stillness stamina — genuinely hard and genuinely trainable at four — cold-region mapping (top of the globe), and respect for patience as a real skill, not a passive one.',
  vocab:'polar bear, arctic, sea ice, camouflage, breathing hole, wait, still',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a polar bear, and this is its home — the arctic, the frozen sea at the top of the world."\n2) Find the bear trick: creamy fur, snowy background. Ask your child to squint — where does the bear end and the ice begin? That vanishing act is called camouflage.\n3) Tell the hunt: seals keep breathing holes in the ice, like front doors. The bear finds one, sits, and waits — silent, statue-still — until a nose pops up.\n4) Compare the hunters: the lion wears gold on gold, the bear wears white on white — different ends of the Earth, the same disappearing trick.\n5) Run the camouflage test and the statue game (below), then close with the map: find the top of the globe — that white crown is the bear\u2019s whole country.',
  say:'"The polar bear lives in the arctic, on the ice at the very top of the world. Its fur is the colour of the snow — so when it waits still as a statue by a seal\u2019s breathing hole, nobody sees it coming. The biggest bear on Earth hunts by being patient."',
  notice:'On this card a big creamy-white polar bear with a long neck, dark eyes and black claws stands on a low slab of sea ice, pale sky and paler sea all around it. The word "arctic" sits below — the hunter standing in its own invisibility cloak.',
  ask:'"Why is the polar bear hard to see on the ice?" "What are breathing holes for?" "What does the bear do at a hole?" "Where on the globe is the arctic?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "Its fur matches the snow — that\u2019s camouflage!" "It waits by the seal\u2019s breathing hole, very still," or the map line: "The arctic is at the top of the world!"',
  real:'Camouflage hunts work anywhere: count the white things against white walls, the brown things against fences — background matching, street edition. Globe time: trace from your home to the top of the world, and let your child place a finger on the bear\u2019s country.',
  play:'Camouflage test: cut a paper bear in white and one in red. Hold each against the white wall, then a busy poster — which bear vanishes, which shouts? Then Statue Hunt: the "bear" must cross the room toward the "seal" (a cushion throne) moving ONLY when the seal\u2019s eyes are closed — freeze when open. Reaching the seal earns the biggest growl of the day.',
  tips:'Keep the hunt honest but gentle: the bear waits because seals are quick and holes are few — patience is not cruelty, it is the job. If your child asks whether the ice ever melts for the bear, answer truthfully and briefly: the arctic ice shifts with the seasons, and the bear follows the ice — the kitchen floor moves, and the bear moves with it.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the polar bear live? Why can\u2019t the seals see it? How does it hunt?" Arctic ice, fur matches snow, statue-still at breathing holes — three answers and the hunter rests.'},
 'bat-cave':{
  title:'The Bat',one:'The upside-down bedroom — a cave that is dark, quiet and safe, a hunter that reads rooms with its ears, and the day-sleeping life of the night shift.',
  seoTitle:'Bat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — Teaching Cave Homes, Echolocation and Night Flying (Class 36)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why bats hang in caves, how they see with their ears through echoes, why they sleep upside down, and an echo game for home.',
  intro:'This lesson closes the class with the strangest address of all: the cave, where the bat sleeps hanging by its feet through the day — then flies out at dusk to hunt the night using nothing but sound.',
  what:'A cave is a room inside rock: dark all day, cool in summer, still and sheltered — no wind, no rain, and no light at all past the entrance. To animals that sleep by day, that darkness is not scary; it is blackout curtains.',
  mean:'The bat sleeps hanging upside down from the cave ceiling, folded in its own wings — feet grab, head drops, day over. At dusk it flies out and hunts in the dark by echolocation: it clicks, the sound bounces off moth or wall or tree, and the echo comes back a picture. Ears as eyes. The cave gives the day\u2019s safe sleep; the night gives the hunt; the bat owns both shifts.',
  know:'Your child needs three bat ideas: bats rest in caves (and similar dark quiet spots) hanging upside down by their feet; they fly at night and find their way by listening to the echoes of their own clicks — seeing with sound; and they are the only mammals that truly fly.',
  why:'The bat corrects two fears at once: caves stop being black holes (they are bedrooms) and bats stop being scary (they are pest-controllers with sonar). Ending the class on the animal that turned darkness into a superpower sends children to bed on the class\u2019s bravest note.',
  dev:'Sound-and-echo physics in game form, fear-to-fascination conversion, upside-down body play with trusted grown-ups, and night-day scheduling — the third shift-worker of the class, after owl and fox.',
  vocab:'bat, cave, hang, upside down, echo, click, dusk',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "This is a bat, and this is its home — the cave."\n2) Read the hang: feet up, head down, wings wrapped like a blanket. Upside down is bat-normal — and handy: drop the grip and flight starts by falling.\n3) Tell the ear-eyes: in pitch dark the bat clicks — tsk-tsk — and the echo bounces back. Moth here, wall there, friend overhead: a sound picture, better in the dark than our eyes are.\n4) Retire the scare: bats eat thousands of bugs a night — the night\u2019s own pest control. Nothing about bats wants your hair; they hear it perfectly and steer away.\n5) Try the echo clap in a hallway, then play the echo game (below) and close the whole class: twenty-two animals, twenty-two homes, every one of them exactly right.',
  say:'"The bat lives in a cave and sleeps hanging upside down by its feet, all day long. At night it flies out and clicks — and the echoes bounce back, drawing a picture in sound: moth over there, wall over here. It sees with its ears! And every night it eats thousands of tiny bugs for us."',
  notice:'On this card a soft grey bat with round ears, a smiling face and neatly folded wings hangs upside down from the rocky ceiling at the mouth of a cave, stones piled at the entrance below. The word "cave" sits below — the night worker, tucked into the quietest bedroom in the class.',
  ask:'"How does the bat sleep — right side up or upside down?" "What does it click, and what comes back?" "When does the bat fly out?" "What do bats eat at night?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "It hangs by its feet with its wings like a blanket," "It sees with its ears — clicks and echoes!" or the brave line: "Bats eat the bugs — they\u2019re the night\u2019s helpers!"',
  real:'Echoes are free: clap once in a stairwell, under a bridge, in an empty hall and listen for the bounce-back — that return is a whisper of the bat\u2019s whole world. Dusk walks gain a mission: bats flicker against the evening sky exactly like fast swallows; point the flicker out and the night shift becomes visible.',
  play:'Echo-location, one-blindfold version: the "bat" sits blindfolded (eyes closed); the "moth" shakes a small box twice from somewhere in the room, then freezes. The bat points to where the sound came from — one click of the box per guess. Harder round: two moths, two corners. The room fills with listening. That is the cave\u2019s gift: darkness worth navigating.',
  tips:'End on the tally, out loud, together: meadow, pond, woodland, forest, eucalyptus, bamboo, rainforest, grassland, savanna, desert, river, coast, ocean, poles, cave — the whole class in one breath, your child\u2019s new map of where everybody lives. And if a bat fear lingers, the cure is the fact: a bat that hunts midges over a pond has zero interest in anything with a hairbrush.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the bat sleep, and how? What does it use to hunt in the dark? When does it fly out?" Cave-upside-down, clicks-and-echoes, at dusk — three answers and the class is complete.'}
};
